From 63ea6e2725085f048dbce5c8e716c3a5e3aa00e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Herman S." <429230+has207@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 23:50:30 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] [CPU/HIR] Select the same vperm half as the backend in the
 int16 fold

Value::Permute's INT16 path and x64's PERMUTE_V128::EmitByInt16 build the
same 8-bit blend mask and then read it inverted. vpblendw takes its second
source where a mask bit is set, so a set bit selects the second table, but
the fold used it to select the first. The fold site passes (src2, src3), so
this picked the wrong half for every lane of every input, and the folded
constant came out the exact complement of what the emitted code computes.

Nothing reached it until VEC128 context promotion. The fold needs the
control and both tables constant, and the tables used to be opaque
load_context dests, so the emitter always ran.

AC4 Black Flag decodes packed vertex normals through a permute that
supplies the per-lane shift counts for the following vector_shr. The wrong
half produced garbage normals, which this now fixes.
---
 src/xenia/cpu/hir/value.cc | 7 ++++---
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/xenia/cpu/hir/value.cc b/src/xenia/cpu/hir/value.cc
index 1255510a9..deb1fbf68 100644
--- a/src/xenia/cpu/hir/value.cc
+++ b/src/xenia/cpu/hir/value.cc
@@ -890,12 +890,13 @@ void Value::Permute(Value* src1, Value* src2, TypeName type) {
       }
     }
 
-    // Blend: select from shuf1 where mask bit is set, shuf2 otherwise
+    // Blend the same way the backends do: vpblendw takes its *second* source
+    // where the mask bit is set, so a set bit selects shuf2.
     for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
       if (mask & (1 << i)) {
-        constant.v128.u16[i] = shuf1.u16[i];
-      } else {
         constant.v128.u16[i] = shuf2.u16[i];
+      } else {
+        constant.v128.u16[i] = shuf1.u16[i];
       }
     }
 
